---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Signup from '../components/Signup.astro';

const lastReviewed = 'October 27, 2026';

const states = [
  { name: 'Maine', since: '2011', size: 'About 951 MW (2025)', rules: 'Closed to new projects in December 2025 (see below).' },
  { name: 'Minnesota', since: '2014', size: 'About 939 MW (2025)', rules: 'Nearly a third of subscribers were low-to-moderate income in a Duke University study.' },
  { name: 'New York', since: '2015', size: 'About 2,600 MW (2025), the largest in the U.S.', rules: 'At least 5% savings. Up to $180 a year off bills for lower-income households through Solar for All.' },
  { name: 'Massachusetts', since: '2014', size: '900 MW yearly cap under SMART 3.0', rules: 'At least 20% savings (40% for low-income). At least 40% of each project’s customers must be low-income. No cancellation fees.' },
  { name: 'Washington, D.C.', since: '', size: '20% of all D.C. solar capacity (2025)', rules: 'Free subscriptions for low-income families through Solar for All.' },
  { name: 'Colorado', since: '2010', size: 'New inclusive program starting 2026 (53.5 MW first round)', rules: 'Lower-income access built into the new program.' },
  { name: 'Illinois', since: '2016', size: 'About 435 MW available in the 2025 to 2026 program year', rules: 'Illinois Solar for All, plus a community-driven option that requires direct local benefits.' },
  { name: 'Maryland', since: '2017', size: 'Grew 30% in 2025', rules: 'A dedicated share of capacity for lower-income households.' },
  { name: 'Rhode Island', since: '2016', size: 'Ranks 9th per resident', rules: 'Subscribers get bill credits for their share of a project’s output.' },
  { name: 'New Jersey', since: '2018', size: 'Authorized to reach 3,250 MW', rules: 'At least 15% savings. At least 51% of each project reserved for low- and moderate-income households.' },
  { name: 'Oregon', since: '2016', size: '161 MW first allocation', rules: 'Fixed credit rates. At least 10% of capacity for low-income households.' },
];
---
<Base
  title="Why community solar? What it delivered in other states"
  description="About 20 states and D.C. have community solar. See the savings, access rules, and results in New York, New Jersey, Massachusetts, Minnesota and more, and what Pennsylvania is missing."
>
  <div class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="eyebrow">Why community solar</div>
      <h1>Why community solar?</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        About 20 states and Washington, D.C. let households sign up for a share of a local solar farm
        and get credits on their electric bill. Hundreds of thousands of families save money this way,
        including renters and people who could never put panels on a roof. Pennsylvania is not one of
        those states yet.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>Four reasons it works</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>1. Savings without a roof.</strong> Subscribers don't buy or install anything. A national
        lab review found community solar can cut bills by 5% to 25%, and several states now guarantee a
        minimum discount: 5% in New York, 15% in New Jersey, and 20% in Massachusetts (40% for low-income
        households).
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>2. Renters and families on tight budgets can join.</strong> Many households can't install
        rooftop solar because they rent, share a building, or can't afford it. In Washington, D.C., free
        subscriptions for low-income families cut the share of income they spend on energy from 13.5% to 8.8%.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>3. Investment in Pennsylvania communities.</strong> A Penn State study of 235 planned
        projects in 48 counties estimated $1.8 billion in economic activity and over 11,000 jobs during
        construction. The study was funded by a community solar industry group.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>4. Cleaner air.</strong> Every unit of solar power means less power from fossil fuel plants.
        By EPA's estimates, each 1,000 kWh of solar avoids roughly 0.7 tons of carbon dioxide, plus
        smog-forming and soot pollution.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>How the leading states compare</h2>
      <p class="prose">
        These are the ten states that lead the country in community solar per resident, plus Oregon, a fast
        grower. Most now guarantee subscribers a minimum discount and reserve space for lower-income households.
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>State</th><th>Since</th><th>Size</th><th>Rules for subscribers</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {states.map((s) => (
              <tr>
                <td><strong>{s.name}</strong></td>
                <td data-label="Since">{s.since}</td>
                <td data-label="Size">{s.size}</td>
                <td data-label="Rules">{s.rules}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
            <tr class="pa">
              <td><strong>Pennsylvania</strong></td>
              <td data-label="Since">None</td>
              <td data-label="Size">0 MW</td>
              <td data-label="Rules">No program yet.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="updated">
        Other states with programs include Connecticut, Hawaii and Vermont, plus Alaska, California, Delaware,
        New Hampshire, New Mexico, Virginia and Washington. Figures last reviewed {lastReviewed}.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>State by state: what it delivered</h2>
      <p><strong>New York.</strong> The largest market in the country. Subscribers get credits right on their utility bill with no extra fees, a guaranteed minimum 5% savings, and a cap of $200 on early cancellation fees. A separate Solar for All program gives lower-income households up to $180 a year off their bills.</p>
      <p><strong>Minnesota.</strong> One of the first states to open a program, in 2014, and the national leader for years. A Duke University study of subscriber data found almost a third of subscribers were low-to-moderate income, even with credit checks in place.</p>
      <p><strong>Massachusetts.</strong> Under rules that took effect in 2025, regular subscribers are guaranteed at least 20% off the value of their credits, and low-income subscribers 40%. Cancellation fees are banned.</p>
      <p><strong>New Jersey.</strong> Pennsylvania's neighbor is the fastest-growing market in the country. Every project must reserve at least 51% of its capacity for low- and moderate-income households, and subscribers save at least 15%. In 2025 the state added 3,000 MW of room to grow. Projects can't be built on preserved farmland or forests, so many sit on rooftops, parking lots and brownfields.</p>
      <p><strong>Illinois.</strong> Started in 2016 and now runs community solar through Illinois Shines and Illinois Solar for All, including a "community-driven" option that requires direct local benefits.</p>
      <p><strong>Maryland.</strong> Began as a pilot in 2017 with a dedicated share for lower-income households. It grew 30% in 2025, one of the fastest rates among tracked states.</p>
      <p><strong>Washington, D.C.</strong> Free subscriptions through the District's Solar for All program cut low-income families' energy burden from 13.5% of income to 8.8%.</p>
      <p><strong>Maine: a lesson in design.</strong> Maine built more community solar per resident than any state, but paid projects generous credits. The program's cost to ratepayers reached $234 million in 2025, and lawmakers closed the program to new projects at the end of that year. Pennsylvania's bill is built differently: its credits don't cover distribution charges, the part of the bill that pays for poles and wires.</p>

      <h2>What Pennsylvania is missing</h2>
      <p>Pennsylvania has zero community solar today. Four of its neighbors already offer it: New Jersey, New York, Maryland and Delaware.</p>
      <p>
        House Bill 504 would change that. It passed the Pennsylvania House in May 2025 and has waited in the
        Senate Consumer Protection and Professional Licensure Committee since May 22, 2025. The current
        session ends November 30, 2026. <a href="/pennsylvania">Read where it stands →</a>
      </p>
      <p>
        Under the bill as passed by the House, projects could be up to 5 MW (20 MW on rooftops and brownfields),
        and at least half of each project's capacity would go to small subscribers like households and farms.
      </p>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Three things you can do:</strong>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="/zip-lookup">Check your ZIP</a> to see your utility and what a subscription could look like.</li>
          <li><a href="#notify">Join the list</a> so you hear first when Pennsylvania opens a program.</li>
          <li><a href="https://www.palegis.us/find-my-legislator" rel="noopener">Find your state senator</a> and tell them you support HB 504.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>

      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <ul class="sources">
        <li><a href="https://ilsr.org/article/energy-democracy/community-solar-tracker/" rel="noopener">ILSR: Community Solar Tracker</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://pv-magazine-usa.com/2026/09/14/state-policy-design-dictates-u-s-community-solar-growth-as-national-contraction-takes-hold/" rel="noopener">pv magazine: State policy design dictates U.S. community solar growth (Sept. 2026)</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://pv-magazine-usa.com/2026/06/04/top-ten-community-solar-states-show-the-power-of-program-design/" rel="noopener">pv magazine: Top ten community solar states (June 2026)</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://pv-magazine-usa.com/2025/09/09/community-solar-makes-subtle-gains-in-capacity-but-bold-moves-in-policy-across-the-u-s/" rel="noopener">pv magazine: Community solar makes subtle gains (Sept. 2025)</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://ilsr.org/article/energy-democracy/illinois-community-solar-program/" rel="noopener">ILSR: Illinois's community solar program</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.nrel.gov/news/program/2021/sharing-the-sun-report.html" rel="noopener">NREL: Sharing the Sun report</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://aese.psu.edu/research/centers/cecd/publications/economic-analyses-including-covid-19-analysis/economic-impact/economic-impact-of-community-solar_sept-2020_psu-cecd.pdf/view" rel="noopener">Penn State: Potential economic impact of community solar in Pennsylvania (2020)</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.epa.gov/node/255385" rel="noopener">EPA: Avoided emission rates from AVERT</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://dukespace.lib.duke.edu/items/a8ca81d7-302e-46ba-a052-7fc0a45e899e" rel="noopener">Duke University: Community solar and Minnesota's low- to moderate-income households</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.mainepublic.org/climate/2025-07-08/maine-eliminates-solar-subsidy-program" rel="noopener">Maine Public: Maine phases out solar subsidy program (July 2025)</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.solarpowerworldonline.com/2026/04/policy-change-virtually-stops-new-community-solar-development-in-maine/" rel="noopener">Solar Power World: Policy change virtually stops new community solar in Maine (April 2026)</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.palegis.us/legislation/bills/2025/hb504" rel="noopener">Pennsylvania General Assembly: HB 504</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Signup />
</Base>

<style>
  .table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; margin: 1em 0; }
  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; background: #fff; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--radius); overflow: hidden; }
  th, td { text-align: left; padding: 14px 16px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); vertical-align: top; }
  th { font-size: .8rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .06em; color: var(--muted); background: var(--green-100); }
  tr.pa td { background: var(--sun-100); }
  .callout ol { margin: .5em 0 0; }
  .sources { font-size: .95rem; }
  @media (max-width: 600px) {
    thead { display: none; }
    table, tbody, tr, td { display: block; width: 100%; }
    tr { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); padding: 12px 14px; }
    td { border: 0; padding: 3px 0; font-size: .95rem; }
    td:first-child { font-size: 1.05rem; padding-bottom: 6px; }
    td[data-label]::before { content: attr(data-label) ": "; font-weight: 700; color: var(--muted); }
    td[data-label]:empty { display: none; }
  }
</style>
